  "query_preview": "..(Sunday through Friday)\nIssued at 412 PM EDT FRI SEP 11 2026\n\nAloft, at 500 mb, prominent features include an upper level\nridge over the Intermountain West and Central Plains, while a\ntrough sits over the Great Lakes and Northeast. In time, the\nridge is expected to move east into the Southeast US including\nthe Tennessee and Ohio Valleys. When this ridge does make its\nway into the region, rising heights will support warmer\ntemperatures, likely into the upper 80s and lower 90s.\nHowever, the last several model runs of the LREF Grand Ensemble\nshow the area holding onto the Northeast trough a little longer\nwhile the mean positioning inches farther south with each\nsuccessive run, thus potentially delaying the warming trend.\n\nCurrently, ensembles have the ridge building into the area\nsometime Monday, cresting over the area Tuesday and Wednesday \nbefore another upper level trough digs out of Central Canada into \nthe Great Lakes later in the week.\n\nFor sensible weather: Sunday morning soundings suggest elevated\nwinds within the lower boundary layer near the surface, as well\nas a surface layer thats not completely saturated. NBM\nguidance depicted areas of fog across the CWA Sunday morning,\nbut due to the above-mentioned mitigating factors, edits were\nmade to confine fog development to the more sheltered river\nvalleys and hollows. Regardless, any fog formation should mix\nout by mid-morning.\n\nThere may also be some isolated shower or thunderstorm chances\nalong the Kentucky/Virginia border Sunday morning. As the\nGreat Lakes trough progresses through the northern Ohio Valley\ninto the Northeast, heights will temporarily fall heading into\nMonday. With the potential for delayed trough timing, offices\nnortheast of the CWA have introduced isolated shower chances\novernight Sunday into Monday, though confidence was still too\nlow to include the same chances across some of our\nnortheastern counties at this time. At the surface, a cold\nfront will move through the area Sunday evening and overnight.\nPatchy river valley fog may develop in the overnight hours.\nThe passage of the cold front is expected to lead to cooler\ntemperatures Monday, ranging from the lower to mid 80s north to \nsouth. With rising heights Tuesday and Wednesday, afternoon high \ntemperatures are expected to warm into the upper 80s to lower 90s\nbefore cooling into the upper 60s at night.\n\nTowards the end of the forecast period, as the secondary trough\nmoves into the Ohio Valley, a cold front at the surface is\nexpected to move into the area sometime Thursday and Friday.\nModels and ensembles start to deviate, leading to the NBM\nsmoothing PoP out over both days. Generally, a 20-45% PoP is\nin the forecast for this time to account for the uncertainty.",
